what is a Thread?
	A thread is the smallest unit of CPU execution. It exists within a process and shares the process's memory and resources with other threads of the same process.
	
Thread memory structure - 
	Process
	│
	├── Code (Shared)
	├── Heap (Shared)
	├── Global Data (Shared)
	│
	├── Thread 1 Stack
	├── Thread 2 Stack
	└── Thread 3 Stack
	
Advantages of Threads
1. Better Responsiveness
	UI remains active while background work happens.
	
2. Resource Sharing
	Threads share memory directly.

3. Faster Creation
	Creating a thread is cheaper than creating a process.

4. Better CPU Utilization
	Multiple CPU cores can execute different threads simultaneously.


What happens if one thread crashes?
	Usually the entire process may crash because all threads share the same address space.
	
A thread is the smallest unit of execution managed by the operating system. Multiple threads can exist within a single process and share the process's memory, code, and resources while maintaining their own stack, registers, and program counter. Threads improve responsiveness, resource sharing, and CPU utilization, making them lighter and more efficient than processes.

