User Threads vs Kernel Threads

What are User Threads?
	User threads are created and managed in user space.
	The OS doesn't know they exist.
	
Advantages of User Threads
	1. Fast Creation
		No system call required.
	2. Fast Context Switching
		Switching happens in user space.
	3. Lower Overhead
		Kernel involvement is minimal.
Disadvantages of User Threads
	Blocking Problem

	Suppose:
		Thread 1 -> File Read
		Thread 2 -> Calculation
		Thread 3 -> UI

	If Thread 1 performs a blocking system call:
		read(file)

	The kernel sees only one execution entity.

	Result:
		Entire Process Blocked

	Even Thread 2 and Thread 3 stop.
	This is the biggest drawback.
		
What are Kernel Threads?
	Kernel threads are managed directly by the operating system.
	Each thread is individually scheduled.
	
Advantages of Kernel Threads
	1. True Parallelism

		On a 4-core CPU:

		Core 1 -> Thread 1
		Core 2 -> Thread 2
		Core 3 -> Thread 3
		Core 4 -> Thread 4

		Threads can run simultaneously.
	2. Better Blocking Behavior
		If:
		Thread 1 -> Waiting for disk
		Only Thread 1 blocks.

		Thread 2 -> Continues
		Thread 3 -> Continues
	3. Kernel Scheduling
		The OS can make smarter scheduling decisions.
		
Disadvantages of Kernel Threads
	Higher Overhead
		Creating a thread requires kernel involvement.

	More Expensive Context Switching
		Switching requires mode transitions.

		User Mode
		↓
		Kernel Mode
		↓
		User Mode

	More work for the OS.
	
User threads are managed by a thread library in user space and are not visible to the operating system kernel. They are lightweight and have low context-switching overhead, but a blocking system call can block the entire process. Kernel threads are managed by the OS kernel, allowing true parallelism and better handling of blocking operations, although they involve higher creation and switching overhead. Modern operating systems generally use kernel threads with a one-to-one threading model.

